Response to the team

Verdict: accept the diagnosis and most of the amendments. Reject D as proposed, because it turns a staged capability into a four-layer build. I would choose a narrower version of D, delivered as decision briefs on a small number of families.

I cannot see the two internal articles the team cites (they are quoted in the message, not attached), so I am treating the overlap with existing problem-management and thematic RCA practice as given. It is a real risk.

What I accept
Team point	Verdict
1. Descriptive, no “so what”	Accept. A family history invites the question “what do I do?”.
2. Decisions not supported by the evidence	Accept. “Enterprise diagnosis” is supportable. “Enterprise treatment” is not.
3. Overlap with existing practice	Accept, and it changes the baseline (below).
4. Mistaking defensibility for value	Partly. The step “descriptive is all we can do” does not follow. Hypotheses can be generated and prioritised without causal claims.
5. “Learning” overstates what is measured	Accept. Retire the name.
Kill test must prove a changed decision	Accept. Surprise is necessary, not sufficient.
Why I reject D as proposed
Layer 4 needs information the data does not hold. “Which option addresses the broadest exposure”, “existing initiatives that overlap” and “funding consequence” depend on cost, in-flight programmes and operating-model knowledge. The schema list shows no cost or effort field. The data can establish that a family exists and what was recorded against it. It cannot rank interventions. That has to come from experts and sit in the brief as their judgement.
Layer 3 smuggles the causal question back in. “Evidence of unresolved vulnerability” after responses is the earlier “despite” in new words. Parts are descriptive and fine (later events inside or outside the recorded response scope, mechanism components with no recorded response). The rest needs the same caveats that sank the intervention study.
It is a four-layer platform. Earlier rounds set the rule not to blend everything into one platform, and the complexity concern was raised twice. Layers 3 and 4 cannot be validated until layers 1 and 2 work.

“Vulnerability” and “portfolio” also overclaim. We would be asserting that exposures exist, and that we hold a portfolio of options.

What I adopt from D

The unit of output is a decision brief, not a family. For each validated family the brief states the decision type, the evidence, the unknowns, and the question for the decision-maker. Content:

Failure family: records, confidence, contradicting evidence, recorded versus inferred links
Recorded responses: issues, actions, themes, scope (local, shared, enterprise-wide)
Descriptive residual view: later events in or outside recorded response scope, and any mechanism component with no recorded response, labelled as a hypothesis
The decision it supports, who owns it, and what additional facts are needed
Expert-supplied intervention options, marked as expert judgement

Decision vocabulary changes. Supportable: commission an enterprise diagnosis, widen the scope of an existing remediation, combine planned diagnostics, investigate a shared dependency, redirect assurance, defer a local action pending diagnosis, raise a missing issue. Not supportable from this data: “fund” and “stop”. Funding would follow from a separate business case, which the brief informs. I would drop “consolidate remediation funding” and “review a repeated remediation theme” from the MVP, since broad themes may be artefacts of the classifier.

Name: Enterprise Failure and Response Intelligence, with the outputs called Enterprise Diagnosis Briefs.

Revised kill test (two weeks, then staged gates)

Baselines now include the existing thematic RCA categories and any problem-management records. The new categories are probably recent, so they cannot cover ten years of history. The text method’s advantage is retrospective, cross-era and cross-object matching. That claim has to be shown, not assumed.

Gate 1, family discovery. As previously proposed: seeded families, text method against label-and-link and RCA-category baselines, expert agreement of at least 0.6, and results beyond sponsor predictions.

Gate 2, decision test (new, required). For at least one validated family the sponsor records:

the decision before seeing the result
the decision after
the exact evidence that changed it
who has authority
what further evidence is needed
the approximate resource consequence, if known

No changed decision means we stop at analysis and do not build briefs.

Gate 3, brief pilot. Briefs for the top five to ten families, with layer 3 limited to the descriptive items above. Stop if sponsors would not act on them.

Stop rules carry over: the baseline finds most of the same families, experts cannot agree, or the sponsor would change nothing under any outcome.

One observation on the process

This is the sixth change of direction, and each has been well argued. The main variable I cannot resolve from here is the one I have asked about twice and not had answered: who is the sponsor, and what decision do they own? Gate 2 cannot run without one. I would stop revising the strategy and run Gate 1 now, because the result will settle more than another round of argument.

Next step: if the team agrees, I will draft the Gate 1 specification (seed family selection, baseline construction including the RCA categories, the expert “same problem” labelling guide, and the sponsor prediction and decision-capture template). Please also confirm whether a business unit field, a cost or effort field, and a problem-management record table exist in the data.
